<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chogha Zanbil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1340 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chogha Zanbil is an archaeological site in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> containing one of the best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurats</a> in the world. It was built around 1340 BCE by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a> as a religious complex. It is the most important surviving monument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil.jpg" alt="Chogha Zanbil" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mobina ghanbaryan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B2%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AA_%DA%86%D8%BA%D8%A7_%D8%B2%D9%86%D8%A8%DB%8C%D9%84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chogha Zanbil, typically translated as 'basket mound', is an Elamite complex in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, about 30 km southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. Its construction date is unclear because the chronology of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a> is uncertain, but it falls in the 14th or 13th century BCE. Its original name was Dur Untash, 'town of Untash', and it was built mainly to honour the great god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a>. It is unlikely that many people besides priests and servants ever lived there. Some scholars infer from its many temples that Untash-Napirisha tried to create a new religious centre, possibly meant to replace Susa, uniting the gods of highland and lowland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Three concentric walls protect the complex. Inscriptions record that the outer wall had seven gates, of which only two have been found. Between the outer and middle walls stood a temple shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napirisha">Napirisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a>, the middle area held eleven temples for other gods, and the outer area royal palaces and a funerary palace with five subterranean royal tombs. Most of the innermost area is taken up by the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, which originally measured 105.2 metres on each side and about 53 metres in height, in five levels, and was crowned with a temple. It has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> core faced with baked bricks, many inscribed with the names of deities in Elamite and Akkadian. It now stands 24.75 metres high.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Building continued after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a>'s death, and the site was occupied until the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> destroyed it in about 645 BCE, though some researchers place the end of occupation in the late 12th century BCE. A geologist doing oil exploration found an inscribed brick and took it to the excavators at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. Roland de Mecquenem excavated the site from 1935, and Roman Ghirshman excavated it in six seasons between 1951 and 1962. In all 5,257 inscribed bricks were found, most in Elamite. In 1979 Chogha Zanbil became the first Iranian site inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, and UNESCO considers its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> the best preserved example of the stepped pyramidal monument.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chogha Zanbil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the thirteenth century BCE Untash-Napirisha built the <b>Chogha Zanbil</b> ziggurat complex, some 105 metres square at its base and now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Untash-Napirisha built a new religious capital, <b>Dur-Untash</b>, with the great ziggurat of Chogha Zanbil, dedicated to Inshushinak and Napirisha.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-elamite-kingdom">Middle Elamite kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
